1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:06,841
This is CommonMesh: a WebMCP-native
workspace for community coordination.

2
00:00:06,841 --> 00:00:09,701
We begin from a deterministic reset:

3
00:00:09,701 --> 00:00:16,546
seven open needs, fifteen available
resources, and no plan or commitments.

4
00:00:16,546 --> 00:00:22,291
An organizer must reconcile quantities,
skills, licences, schedules,

5
00:00:22,291 --> 00:00:24,680
travel, and accessibility.

6
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:27,705
The agent handles that comparison work,

7
00:00:27,705 --> 00:00:35,251
while CommonMesh keeps every real-world
commitment visible and human-controlled.

8
00:00:35,251 --> 00:00:41,130
Nine structured tools expose the domain
directly: seven reads and two writes.

9
00:00:41,130 --> 00:00:45,869
The agent can inspect, validate, stage,
and commit.

10
00:00:45,869 --> 00:00:52,304
But approval, reset, and
resource availability remain human-only.

11
00:00:52,304 --> 00:00:56,508
The agent reads stable identifiers
and structured constraints

12
00:00:56,508 --> 00:00:58,888
instead of guessing from pixels.

13
00:00:58,888 --> 00:01:04,390
It validates the assignments, then
stages—not executes—the proposal

14
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:09,648
through the same state store
used by the interface.

15
00:01:09,648 --> 00:01:16,510
The proposal covers every need and exposes
its assignments, metrics, revision,

16
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:20,487
validation, and a SHA-256 digest.

17
00:01:20,487 --> 00:01:24,102
Notice that projected coverage
is one hundred percent

18
00:01:24,102 --> 00:01:29,669
while live coverage is still zero.
Nothing has executed.

19
00:01:29,669 --> 00:01:35,119
Even when explicitly asked to commit
early, the domain layer rejects the write

20
00:01:35,119 --> 00:01:39,207
with APPROVAL_REQUIRED
and records the blocked attempt.

21
00:01:39,207 --> 00:01:41,867
The agent cannot approve itself.

22
00:01:41,867 --> 00:01:45,300
I approve this exact digest
in the visible interface.

23
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:47,722
Approval still does not execute it.

24
00:01:47,722 --> 00:01:50,676
Now the agent commits only
that approved digest.

25
00:01:50,676 --> 00:01:59,575
CommonMesh recomputes it, revalidates
the plan, and activates the assignments.

26
00:01:59,575 --> 00:02:03,657
Real plans change. As the human,
I mark the assigned van unavailable.

27
00:02:03,657 --> 00:02:12,243
Only its dependent assignment is disrupted
—the other seven commitments stay active.

28
00:02:12,243 --> 00:02:18,372
The agent inspects the changed revision
and stages the smallest valid repair.

29
00:02:18,372 --> 00:02:23,902
CommonMesh proves the impact:
seven existing assignments preserved,

30
00:02:23,902 --> 00:02:28,287
one replaced, and full projected
coverage restored.

31
00:02:28,287 --> 00:02:33,771
After a second exact-digest approval,
the agent commits the repair.

32
00:02:33,771 --> 00:02:38,072
We end at seven of seven needs covered,
eight active assignments,

33
00:02:38,072 --> 00:02:41,426
and a complete human-and-agent
audit trail.

34
00:02:41,426 --> 00:02:47,921
CommonMesh gives agents useful autonomy
without giving away human authority.
